        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(q+r)\\left(\\dfrac qr+\\dfrac rq+1\\right)=1$.\n\nUse $P(A\\cap B)=2p(q+r)$ and substitute the probabilities from parts (a) and (b) into the independence criterion.",
          "worked": "The event $A\\cap B$ consists of $PQ,QP,PR,RP$, so $P(A\\cap B)=2p(q+r)$.\nLet $s=q+r=1-p$. Independence gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2ps&=(2p-p^2)\\,2(ps+qr)\\\\s&=(2-p)(ps+qr)\\\\s&=(1+s)((1-s)s+qr)\\\\s&=s-s^3+(1+s)qr\\\\s^3&=(1+s)qr\\\\s^3-sqr&=qr\\\\s(s^2-qr)&=qr\\\\s\\left(\\frac{s^2-qr}{qr}\\right)&=1\\\\(q+r)\\left(\\frac qr+\\frac rq+1\\right)&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThe divisions are valid because $p,q,r>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      },
